  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/19-halfbeaks/hemirhamphodon_tengah_en/",
      "html": "<p>This pretty little fish is a challenge for experienced hobbyists. Maximum length of the fish is about 6 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/19-halfbeaks/hemirhamphodon_tengah_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species originates from Borneo. It was first collected in the province of Kalimantan Tengah (Central Kalimantan) on Borneo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/19-halfbeaks/hemirhamphodon_tengah_en/",
      "html": "<p>In contrast to the widespread prejudice, halfbeaks of the genus Hemirhamphodon do not appear in brackish water; they are strictly bound to very soft and acidic water! These fishes are commonly found along with chocolate gouramis and small rasboras.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/19-halfbeaks/hemirhamphodon_tengah_en/",
      "html": "<p>The fish are strictly surface-dwellers. They are not very demanding regarding food, but never take food from the bottom. So they should be fed with flakes and different types of live food. Small fruit flies (Drosophila) are a delicacy for Hemirhamphodon.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/19-halfbeaks/hemirhamphodon_tengah_en/",
      "html": "<p>The other species of Hemirhamphodon are livebearers. Not so H. tengah: this is an egg-laying species. Breeding H. tengah is in most respects similar to breeding killifishes of the Aphyosemion-type. The females like to spawn in java moss, peat or in artificial breeding mops made of wool.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/19-halfbeaks/hemirhamphodon_tengah_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lexikon. Hemirhamphodon: from ancient Greek, means about \"with teeth on the halfbeak\". tengah: after the province Kalimantan tengah on Borneo, where the species has been collected initially.</p><p>Suggestion of a common name: Egglaying halfbeak</p>"
    }
  ],
